    ('h2', 'Honest boundaries of the real-intelligence layer'),
    ('p', 'Four boundaries are part of the record. First, embeddings: the platform SDK exposes no embeddings endpoint, so T10 still routes to Ollama\'s nomic-embed-text on a real machine and to the deterministic mock in this sandbox - search works, provenance says exactly which leg ran, and a ZAI embeddings leg slots into models.yaml the day the platform exposes one. Second, determinism: a real model does not produce byte-identical outputs across runs, which is why the golden-set eval stays pinned to the deterministic leg by default and a new --live flag runs the suite against real providers informationally - measuring drift is the point, not failing CI on it. Third, the cloud STT egress: it is off by default and stays off in the delivered state; the evidence call in the library was produced with the toggle on and the toggle was then returned to false, which is the exact contract the Settings disclosure card describes. Fourth, rate limits: the platform throttles, so bursty multi-call processing backs off politely; a personal pipeline processes one call at a time through six tasks and stays well inside the observed limits.'),

    ('h1', 'Part II - The Interface, Elevated'),
    ('p', 'The instruction "make the interface 10/10 so people love using it" needed a yardstick, so the build gained a measurement loop: every screen was screenshotted in a real browser and audited by a vision model prompted as a strict principal designer (ex-Linear, ex-Arc) with instructions to score craft out of ten and name the three highest-impact fixes. Three full iterations ran. The first audit scored the v1.1 interface between 4 and 6.5 (average about 5.3) and its critiques were specific and repeatable: weak typographic hierarchy, inconsistent spacing rhythm, visual noise in the footer and sidebar debug block, unpolished components (native-feeling selects, cluttered pipeline pips), and empty states that lacked any craft. Each iteration fixed the named issues at the root and re-measured; the average moved to about 6.2 and then about 6.5, at which point the critiques turned contradictory between rounds (one round demands heavier titles, the next lighter) - the noise floor of the critic, and the signal to stop chasing it and verify structure instead: zero console errors, exact mobile behavior, working interactions.'),

    ('h2', 'What changed, screen by screen'),
    ('p', 'The system layer first: globals.css gained a craft tier - layered warm shadows for depth, the hero radial wash, the brand gradient, a shimmer skeleton, a gentle view-rise animation for route changes, micro-caps section labels, a softer focus ring, and warm theming for the command palette. The primitives were rebuilt around a single type hierarchy: PageHeader (display title, muted sub, right-aligned actions) now opens every screen; StatCard grew icons, count-up animated numbers, tone rings and hover lift; the StageRail - the audit\'s single most-criticized component - was redesigned from labeled pips into a connected segment bar with tooltips and an accessible label; EmptyState gained an icon circle and a clear action; SkeletonList replaced every "Loading…" string. The shell gained the brand mark, live status chips (AI live/local, routing mode, queue depth) replacing the debug text block, a decluttered footer with keyboard hints, and a command palette on Cmd+K covering navigation and the two primary actions. The login screen became a front door: radial wash, brand, one-line pitch, and trust chips stating the privacy stance. The dashboard opens with a greeting header and, when work waits, a gradient review-queue banner; its four KPI cards count up and click through to their screens. The inbox rebuilt its rows around title-primary/meta-secondary hierarchy with pill-styled error messages. The upload dropzone gained the affordances the audit asked for (icon, hover wash, drag feedback). The live console got its hierarchy fixed - semibold battlecard names, spaced cards, grouped controls - plus a loading skeleton and the start-transition race fix. The coaching screen replaced its flat calibrating card with a crafted empty state, put a gradient area under its trend line, and lost both a rendering bug (a stray parenthesis in the table header) and three React DOM warnings (SVG attributes needed camelCase). The library opens with a hero header and a larger search field; the settings providers grid moved to an auto-fill layout with friendly provider names, the ZAI routing toggle inline, and the cloud-STT disclosure card in warning tones.'),

